                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "You'd think with a name like this the game would nab a jab to mentioned topics but the joke is that the game's name is long. The game is a point and click at its simplest form, no random interactables, no dialogues and no inventory, which is labelled as one of it's \"key features\" when really it's a lack thereof, and most boss \"battles\" end in one swing. I've played corporate PR flash games with more depth than this in the past, ironic. Even if you're playing this just for the humor aspect for the journey, it never goes past of what you usually see on children's shows. About the Shakespare play, this game's plot is more akin to the one of Super Mario Brothers for NES and the characters are cardboard cutouts of necessary roles, the evil villain, damsel in distress, henchmen and hero, with just their names adapted from the famous play, so you shouldn't consider this just because it's named Hamlet either. It's cheap. It's short. And that's all there is. Go and play To Be or Not To Be by Ryan North for a proper adaptation. Nothing to see here.",
